## Release log

- **2026-08-19** — Go 1.27; `GOEXPERIMENT=jsonv2` retired; linters pinned
  around a toolchain they do not yet support; stdlib modernization pass;
  hujson updated.
- **2026-09-02** — toolchain go1.27.1; no module moved; `go fix` clean;
  staticcheck unpinned, then it and `go vet` folded into golangci-lint —
  `make lint` is one command over one binary.

## Key decisions

- **WebSocket library: `github.com/coder/websocket`.** Context-aware,
  zero-dependency, net/http-native. Chosen over gorilla/websocket (no
  contexts, chattier API) and gobwas/ws (lower level than this needs).
- **Persistence: SQLite via `modernc.org/sqlite`**, pure Go, no cgo, with
  the single-writer/many-reader pool split — same as the sibling
  kikapcsologo backend, so the tooling and query conventions carry over.
- **Hand-written SQL in `internal/model/`**, no code generation.
- **Fan-out is per-channel, never global.** Each channel owns its member
  set and does its own broadcast; the hub's map is only touched on
  join/part/lookup.
- **Backpressure never propagates into the hub.** A full outbound buffer
  disconnects that client; it must not block the sender.
- **Swappable `Broadcaster` interface in `internal/broadcast`.** The fan-out
  is the component most likely to be rewritten, so alternatives are added
  to one table and are measured by the existing tests and benchmarks
  without touching a caller.
- **Ring will not throttle senders; MapChan accidentally did.** MapChan's
  O(N) send path was backpressure by side effect, so Ring is the one that
  needs enough buffer and an actual rate limiter. `internal/ratelimit` is
  load-bearing, not a nicety.
- **Ring capacity has to be sized for the lag SPREAD, not the average.** At
  10k subscribers with 1000 senders, 256 slots collapses to 0.07% delivery
  and 4096 gives 99.8% at 7.7 ns/msg — nine times faster than MapChan.
  Affordable only because the storage is shared: 4096 slots is 64KB total,
  where the same slack per subscriber would be 655MB. The server default
  Capacity is 4096 for this reason. Measured in
  `BenchmarkFanoutChattyCapacity`.
- **The fan-out read path is lock-free: `SeqRing`, not `Ring`.** A pointer
  per slot means the writer replaces and never mutates, so a reader holds
  what it loaded safely with the GC as the grace period; `begin`/`published`
  bracket the write so a whole batch is validated with one load. 2x on
  paced delivery, up to 6x on churn under load and flat in the sender
  count, for 20ns and one 24-byte allocation per broadcast. **The win is
  proportional to reader contention**, so busy rooms and join/part gain and
  quiet rooms are a wash — worth knowing before optimising this again.
  `Ring` stays as the reference implementation.
- **The fan-out is not the bottleneck and further fan-out work will not
  move the server.** Profiled at both loads: under 5% of CPU healthy, under
  1% collapsing, no attributable allocation. The server is `write(2)` (two
  thirds of CPU) and `coder/websocket`'s per-`Write` deadline context
  (~700 bytes per delivered message, 87% of garbage under overload). The
  library has no `SetWriteDeadline`, so that allocation needs the library
  changed, not the server. Spend optimisation effort there or on a second
  machine, not here.
- **The server now bounds writes with `Conn.SetWriteDeadline`, on a pinned
  fork of `coder/websocket`, and that pin is temporary.** `go.mod` carries a
  directory `replace` to the fork with the revert instructions beside it;
  the code is vendored, so CI and a fresh clone build without the fork and
  only `make vendor` needs it. This is the one place the repo knowingly
  depends on unreleased upstream code — revert it the moment
  `SetWriteDeadline` is in a release. Why it was worth doing:
- **`SetWriteDeadline` on `coder/websocket`'s `Conn` is the one
  optimisation left that is worth real effort — upstream, not as a fork.**
  Built and measured: garbage per delivered message goes from 729 bytes to
  **4.9**, CPU for identical work drops 12%, and mean latency 15%, at the
  STEADY load where every other change in this repo has been a wash. The
  write path ends up allocating nothing; what is left is connection setup.
  A deadline is an instant, and an instant needs no allocation, where a
  context needs a `WithTimeout` in the caller and an `AfterFunc` in the
  library. Not adopted only because a fork leaves `govulncheck`'s view of
  RFC 6455 parsing code. **The submittable PR — patch, tests, written
  message, reproduction steps — is in `code-websocket-pr/`**; the numbers
  and the two gotchas are in `state/loadgen.md`.
- **The cheaper variant, memoising the armed context, is not worth it.**
  It removes the per-frame amplification under overload and changes
  nothing at steady load, where batches are one frame. Superseded by the
  above.
- **A drop happens at exactly the promised slack, in every
  implementation.** Survive at `size` behind, die at `size+1`, asserted
  deterministically rather than inferred from delivery ratios — which is
  what lets one implementation's capacity findings transfer to another's.
- **`sync.Cond` was measured against the ring's notify channel and lost.**
  It removes the per-broadcast allocation and delivery is a wash, but it
  cannot wake one waiter, so a part wakes the whole room — 13x on a
  thousand sleepers, growing with room size. Detail in
  `state/broadcast.md`; do not re-litigate without reading it.
- **Fan-out benchmarks count deliveries, not calls.** A drop-on-full
  broadcaster looks fastest when it fails fastest, so every fan-out
  benchmark reports `delivered/sent` and `drops/op` next to `ns/op`.
  Comparisons are made with a paced sender: an implementation whose send
  path is O(1) has nothing pacing it against its receivers and will lap
  them instead of feeding them.
- **Filters are a chain, with text hygiene built in.** `internal/filter`
  runs UTF-8 validity and a zalgo limit in front of any hook filter,
  because a message that is not valid text breaks clients whatever
  anybody's moderation opinions are. Invalid UTF-8 is reachable in
  practice: MessagePack clients send binary frames and nothing validates
  those.
- **Moderation defaults to nobody being a moderator.** `Authorizer` is the
  one hook whose default is deny, and it is asked per scope.
- **`Directory.Resolve` turns a nick into an identity**, which is what lets
  moderation act on people who are not connected — without it a ban cannot
  be lifted, since the person it barred is by definition not there.
- **Moderation survives a restart through the `Sanctions` hook** (Record +
  Active), separate from `Recorder`. A failing `Active` stops the server:
  starting without knowing who is banned means letting them in.
- **Moderation is scoped by the command's channel**: named acts there,
  empty acts server-wide, and a lookup checks both. A channel mute does
  not reach private messages; a server-wide one does.
- **Rate limiting is mechanism in the core, policy in a hook.** Token
  buckets live in `internal/ratelimit`; the `Limiter` hook is asked once
  per connection and once per channel for the numbers and never sees a
  message. Unlimited is the default and costs a nil check. `Limits.Key`
  chooses the scope: empty is per connection, non-empty shares one
  refcounted bucket across an account — and such a bucket outlives its
  connections, or reconnecting would refill it.
- **Observability is built in, not hooked.** `internal/metrics` is a
  hand-rolled Prometheus-text registry; pprof and `/metrics` share a
  loopback-bound listener (`DebugAddr`). Policy is a hook; the server
  describing itself is not.
- **Who may CONNECT and who may TALK are different questions, answered by
  different hooks.** `Authenticator` refuses the connection; `Filter`
  refuses the message. A server where anonymous visitors watch and only
  registered users speak is the gap between them, and needs no core support
  — a lurker is an ordinary connection whose `MSG` and `PRIVMSG` are
  refused with `needlogin`.
- **Everything that is not moving bytes between sockets is a hook.**
  `internal/hook` defines auth, chatter data, message filtering and
  persistence as interfaces; the core imports the interfaces and never an
  implementation. The zero `Hooks` is a working server.
- **The JSON codec is `encoding/json/v2`, so the module needs `go 1.27`
  to build at all.** It shipped in 1.27 and is gated on the `go` directive
  rather than the old `GOEXPERIMENT=jsonv2`. Decode is 2.2x faster with
  one allocation instead of eight; encode is ~45% slower, and that belongs
  to the engine rather than the API — v1 on the same engine is just as
  slow, and since 1.27 there is no other engine — which is what makes
  calling v2 directly the right choice. Net per message is about 8% and
  half the garbage; the strictness (invalid UTF-8, duplicate members) is
  the better half of the deal. Optional scalars are tagged `omitzero`
  because v2's `omitempty` would put `"sent":false` on the wire.
- **A linter that exits 0 is not the same as a clean tree.** Go releases
  run ahead of the linters, and `cmd/staticcheck` answered a toolchain it
  could not read with a few lines and a success status. That binary is
  gone — staticcheck runs inside golangci-lint now, which fails loudly —
  but the habit it bought stays: prove the analysers fire before believing
  "0 issues".
- **The wire format is negotiated, not configured.** `proto.Codec` is an
  interface over bytes, selected by WebSocket subprotocol. MessagePack
  preferred, JSON the default for anything that did not ask. A ring holds
  encoded bytes, so there is one broadcaster per codec. Preferring msgpack
  is now measured rather than assumed: 4.2% less server CPU for identical
  delivered traffic, mostly `write(2)` moving fewer bytes.
- **The verb lives inside the encoded document**, so an inbound frame costs
  one decode. `proto.Command` is a flat union of every inbound field; one
  struct per verb would reintroduce the second parse. This left the codecs
  as nothing but Marshal/Unmarshal — there is no framing code.
- **Load is generated from another process, as a plain client.**
  `cmd/loadgen` dials like anybody else and counts what lands on its own
  sockets; it never reads a server counter. A benchmark inside the server
  shares its scheduler, allocator and GC and then reports on them as the
  server's — and cannot be aimed at another machine.
- **CI runs the Makefile, not `go`.** `.woodpecker.yaml` shells out to
  `make` for every step so whatever flags a step needs are stated in
  exactly one place. It skips `make vendor`: a CI run checks the tree it was handed
  rather than rewriting it.
- **The write pump takes batches, not single messages.** `Sub.Recv` fills a
  slice, so one wakeup drains everything pending and can coalesce it into a
  single socket write. This is what makes shared-storage fan-out possible
  at all — and it is why cost per delivery *falls* 3.5x as load rises,
  measured 13.2µs at 50k deliveries/s down to 3.8µs at 773k.
- **The capacity limit is the kernel's network path, and it is a product.**
  ~600k deliveries/s on a 4-vCPU guest at p50 18ms, where the box is 0% idle
  with 69% of it system time and the process itself is 2.9 of 4 cores. Size
  a deployment by senders × rate × room size, not by connection count, and
  check the NIC first: 1GbE runs out within a few percent of the same place.
- **Every goroutine is started inside `pprof.Do`**, labelled `task=<role>`
  from a closed set. A new long-lived goroutine gets a label and a name in
  the `debug.go` const block, in the same commit — an unlabelled one is
  invisible in a profile that everything around it is visible in. Values
  stay closed for the same reason metric labels do.

## Removed

_(nothing yet)_

## User preferences

- Linus Torvalds persona when writing code; avoid needless complexity.
- Every logical step is its own commit, in the documented kernel style.
- `make pre-commit` (vendor + lint + `-race` tests) before every commit.
